From 82c3b418880f426ee695811cca713506ad1d22c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:27:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Kanban submitter books its own exit 76 when no owner
 can

Incident: example-tools/t_01a197166756bd53 run 207 on hermes-hub, 2026-11-13. The owner gateway
4111315 was alive but wedged 20:03-20:12:35 (killed by the loop-liveness watchdog). The bound
interpreter 4117653 wrote two exit-1 receipts (events 10270 "couldn't save this conversation", 10271
`Worker failed: WorkerPersistenceError: owner_unavailable`). The outer submitter 3995469 printed
`Kanban owner restarted: Kanban owner silent for 283s: Gateway connection failed; no local fallback`
and exited rc=76, but that line exists only in the card's shared task log: run 207 has no
worker_result with exit_code 76 from the submitter. At 20:15:30 the observer booked run 207 `crashed`
from event 10271 (source_result_event_id 10271, failure 1 of 2, infrastructure false).

Five whys:
1. Why was the run booked from the inner exit 1? `reclaim_proof`/`result_exit_code` take the exit from
   the causally selected modern receipt (`kanban_failed_result_identity.attributable_events`); the
   exit trailer in the log is read only when no exit code is known
   (`kanban_db_dispatch._classify_dead_worker_exit`). Evidence: crashed event 10272 payload.
2. Why was there no 76 receipt? `kanban_worker_client._submit`'s `except OwnerRestartLost` printed the
   line and returned 76 without booking anything, while the sibling refusal exits (69 owner
   unavailable, owner down, 1) book theirs via `_book_refusal` -> `record_submitter_result`
   (fb794abb716). The board is a local SQLite file the submitter writes directly, so an unreachable
   owner does not stop it; it simply never tried.
3. Why did nobody else book it? The 76 receipt is written by the owner itself, and only when it stops
   cleanly (`gateway/session_kanban_restart.record_restart_interruption`; run 207's earlier receipts
   10251 and 10261 are exactly that). A wedged or killed owner never runs that path.
4. Why wasn't it caught? The 76 tests cover the client returning 76 and the sweep reading a reaped 76
   or the log trailer of an unbound legacy run (test_kanban_worker_owner_restart.py), and the
   owner-written receipt (test_kanban_owner_restart_failed_receipt.py). No test combined a modern bound
   receipt with a submitter-only 76.

Decision (outer 76 vs inner exit 1): the submitter's 76 wins, as the owner-written 76 already does over
a failed child (test_native_owner_supersedes_failed_child). The submitter raises OwnerRestartLost only
after the owner admitted the turn and then never settled it (silent past the restart budget, refused
to continue a checkpoint, or unknown after a restart); a healthy owner relays the interpreter's
terminal receipt and the submitter exits with that code instead.

Change: `record_submitter_owner_restart` (kanban_worker_log) books the submitter's 76 under the same
attempt fence as `record_submitter_result` (task running this run under this claim, the submitter is
the task's worker, run row agrees), with the full owner-restart witness
(`owner_restart_identity_fields`: spawn identity + every prior result/binding), the only shape the
classifier accepts for an owner role. An existing interpreter receipt does not suppress it; an
unwitnessed launch writes nothing (an unmarked later result would veto reclaim). Never raises: a
store failure keeps exit 76 and prints "receipt could not be recorded".

Proof: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_outer_owner_restart_receipt.py runs the real `main()` in a
subprocess as the task's worker with a real bound interpreter subprocess. RED before (receipts
[1, 1] / [1], no 76), GREEN after (10 passed): run 207's two receipts, the turn-only receipt #2658
does not cover, and a card-text receipt, each through dispatcher and observer, all requeued
`reclaimed` with failures 0 and owner_restart_lost sourced from the submitter receipt; no inner
receipt; a submitter that is not the run's worker or whose claim moved books nothing; a receipt-store
failure still exits 76.

Not tested: a live hub replay with a wedged owner; a submitter racing the owner's own late 76 booking
(both are complete witnessed 76s; the classifier takes one); NFS/replica boards.
